tial pung/kong, but we have no exposed melds yet.  \n- B7 pair, no other pair except possible later.  \n- Many W tiles (W1-W3 close sequence, W5-W7 close sequence) \u2014 W tiles could aim for flush (Wan-only hand) since we have many W1\u2013W7.  \n- Also mixed suits present: T, B.  \n\n**Current hand evaluation for direction:**  \n- Full flush (Wan only) needs discarding all T and B tiles. That would require drawing more W tiles. Possible but uncertain.  \n- Half flush possible if we keep W plus honors, but no honors yet.  \n- All simples possible (no terminals/honors) if we discard W1, W9-like terminals, but here W1 is terminal, so to keep all simples we must discard W1 and T3/T7 possibly.  \n- Or we can aim for normal hand and try to form sequences and pung quickly.  \n\nFrom the history:  \n- Previous player has exposed Chow W7 W8 W9, meaning they used W8 from discard, so W8 is less available (only 3 left originally, 1 used). W9 also less available (3 left originally).  \n- Others discarding terminals/honors early; F tiles being discarded a lot.  \n\nUnshown tiles count:  \n- For Wan suit, W7 (3 left), W8 (3 left), W9 (3 left). So some W8-9 still possible but reduced.  \n- W1-W6 all 4 copies each, so still plentiful.  \n\nIf I aim for **Full Flush in Wan**, I must discard T3, T4, T7, B5, B7, and draw W tiles to form melds. But losing T4 pung is wasteful \u2014 maybe not best.  \n\nIf I aim for **quick win with mixed hand**, I could keep T4 pung ready and W sequences. Currently I need one more pair (only B7 pair, T4 triple not pair).  \n\n**Promising discard candidates:**  \n1. **B7** \u2014 eliminates a pair, but B7 pair is my only pair currently. Dangerous? Maybe keep pair.  \n2. **T7** \u2014 isolated terminal tile in T suit, no nearby tiles in hand.  \n3. **W1** \u2014 terminal, blocks all simples but starts sequences: W2 W3 need W1 or W4, keeping W1 for 1-2-3 chow possible if draw W4. But W1 is not contributing to other melds yet except possible 123 chow if draw W4.  \n4. **W5/W6** \u2014 in middle of W5-W7 potential chow; W5-W6 need W4 or W7. Currently W7 is single.  \n5. **W2** or **W3** \u2014 but they are part of W1-2-3 potential chow if W1 stays.  \n\nIf I discard **W1**:  \n- Lose terminal, helps All Simples possibility.  \n- Keep T4 triple and B7 pair, W2 W3 still can combine with W4 (draw) to make chow.  \n\nIf I discard **T7**:  \n- Reduces terminals, keeps W1-2-3 possible chow, and W5-6-7 possible chow, T4 ready for pung.  \n\nBut W5-6-7: W5, W6, W7 all singles; for 567 chow need either W4 W5 W6 or W5 W6 W7 or W6 W7 W8, currently I have W5 W6 W7 so that's already a consecutive set \u2014 oh wait, they are W5, W6, W7, yes that is 5-6-7 sequence already! So W5, W6, W7 can be a chow if sorted. So I shouldn't break that.  \n\nActually let's check:  \nCurrent hand: W1, W2, W3, W5, W6, W7, T3, T4 T4 T4, T7, B5, B7 B7.  \n\nPossible melds in hand:  \n- (W1, W2, W3) chow if keep them.  \n- (W5, W6, W7) chow if keep them.  \n- T4 pung.  \n\nRemaining: T3, T7, B5, B7 pair. Need to get rid of T3 T7 B5, form another meld and keep a pair (B7 already pair).  \n\nThat's 2 chows + 1 pung + 1 pair = 4 melds? Actually that's 3 melds: Chow W1-3, Chow W5-7, Pung T4, and B7 pair \u2014 that's 4 sets, that's a complete hand! But it needs all to be valid:  \nWe must have exactly 14 tiles: Currently we have 13 hand tiles. Wait, let's count:  \nW1,W2,W3,W5,W6,W7 = 6 tiles  \nT3, T4x3, T7 = 5 tiles  \nB5, B7x2 = 3 tiles  \nTotal = 14? 6+5=11, +3=14, yes.  \n\nSo if I treat:  \nMeld1: W1 W2 W3 (chow)  \nMeld2: W5 W6 W7 (chow)  \nMeld3: T4 T4 T4 (pung)  \nMeld4: B7 B7 (pair)  \n\nThat uses W1..W7, T4x3, B7x2. Leftover tiles: T3, B5, T7 \u2014 3 extra tiles! Wait that\u2019s wrong: total hand tiles count 14 includes T3, T7, B5? Yes, those 3 are extra.  \n\nWe can't have more than 14 in final hand.  \n\nSo my current hand is 14 tiles, I need to discard 1 now. Discarding 1 of T3, T7, B5, B7, W1, etc.  \n\nSo I have **too many tiles**? Wait the game says \"Your hand tiles are: [W1: 1; W2: 1; W3: 1; W5: 1; W6: 1; W7: 1; T3: 1; T4: 3; T7: 1; B5: 1; B7: 2]\".  \nLet's count:  \nW1-W3 = 3  \nW5,W6,W7 = 3  \nT3 = 1  \nT4x3 = 3  \nT7 = 1  \nB5 = 1  \nB7x2 = 2  \nTotal = 3+3+1+3+1+1+2 = 14, yes.  \n\nSo I must discard 1 now from valid actions:  \n\nIf I keep the two W chows and T4 pung and B7 pair:  \nThat\u2019s W1-3, W5-7, T4x3, B7x2. That\u2019s 3+3+3+2 = 11 tiles. Leftover from hand: T3, T7, B5 (3 tiles), but hand must have 14 total, so leftover 3 tiles are T3, T7, B5 which are useless singles \u2014 so that\u2019s not a complete hand (need 4 melds+pair).  \n\nWe actually need 4 melds+pair = 14 tiles. My two W chows + T4 pung = 3 melds (9 tiles) + B7 pair (2 tiles) = 11 tiles, plus 3 singles T3,T7,B5 which are 3 tiles total 14. I\u2019m missing one meld.  \n\nSo I\u2019m one meld short. Which meld could I make? Possibly T3 T4 T5 chow (need T5) or B5 B6 B7 chow (need B6).  \n\nSo strategy:  \nDiscard a tile that doesn\u2019t break potential chows/pungs and leaves flexibility.  \n\nT7 is isolated terminal, no other T except T3 and T4.  \nB5 is isolated, only near B7 but B7 is pair.  \n\nPotential: If draw B6, B5 B6 B7 chow. If draw T5, T3 T4 T5 chow.  \n\nBut we have T4 triple, so using T4 in chow would break pung. So better to keep T4 pung.  \n\nSo possible chow from singles:  \n- T3 needs T1 T2 or T4 T5 or T2 T4, but T4 is triple (fixed pung), so T3 can only make chow with T1 T2 (both far) or with T5 and a T4? No, T4 is tied in pung. So T3 is hard to use.  \n- B5 can make chow with B3 B4 or B4 B6 or B6 B7. B7 is pair, so B5-B7 needs B6.  \n\nBest discard now: **T7** (terminal, no neighbors in hand, no loss of potential chow in hand).  \n\n**Valid actions**: Play W1; Play B7; Play W2; Play W6; Play T7; Play T4; Play W7; Play W5; Play B5; Play W3; Play T3.  \n\nT4, W5, W6, W7 are part of chows/pungs, not discard. B7 is only pair, keep. W1-3 chow possible, keep. So T7, B5, T3 candidates among singles.  \nT3 has more potential because if draw T2, we have T2 T3 T4? But T4 is pung, so T3 can\u2019t be used with T4. So T3 also dead.  \n\nActually B5 can be used with B7 pair and B6 draw (B5 B6 B7 chow) and then B7 pair gone, need another pair. Risky.  \n\nGiven potential for W flush, maybe discard T7 now.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:**  \nBest discard is T7 \u2014 it\u2019s a terminal with no immediate connections in hand, keeping all W sequences intact, keeping T4 pung ready, keeping B7 pair, keeping possible B5-B6-B7 chow later if needed.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nT7 is the most isolated tile and least useful for forming melds given current hand structure; discarding it preserves flexibility for both W flush and standard hand, and doesn\u2019t break any potential chows in hand.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T7"}
