nd pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be exposed, and one tile of the pair is the 14th winning tile from other players.\\n        * Two Concealed Kongs: A hand with two concealed kongs.\\n        * Two Dragon Pungs: A hand with two pungs or kongs of dragon tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 4 points:\\n        * Outside Hand: A hand with terminals (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles in every meld and the pair.\\n        * Fully Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be concealed, and the winning tile is drawn by the winner.\\n        * Two Melded Kongs: A hand with two exposed kongs.\\n        * Last Tile: The winning tile is the last tile of the same tile that has not been revealed to all players, which means three of the same tile has been exposed in melds or discarded on the table.\\n    Patterns worth 2 points:\\n        * Dragon Pung: A hand with a pung or kong of dragon tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Prevalent Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the prevalent wind of this game.\\n        * Seat Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the seat wind of the current player.\\n        * Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players except the winning tile, which means that all melds must be concealed, while the winning tile is seized from other players.\\n        * All Chows: A hand with four chows and a pair of suited tiles.\\n        * Tile Hog: A hand with four identical tiles without forming a kong. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple tiles meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Pung: A hand with two pungs or kongs in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Concealed Pungs: A hand with two concealed pungs or kongs.\\n        * Concealed Kong: A hand with a concealed kong.\\n        * All Simples: A hand with no terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 1 points:\\n        * Pure Double Chow: A hand with two identical chows. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Chow: A hand with two chows in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Short Straight: A hand with two chows in the same suit with successive numbers, like 234 and 567. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Terminal Chows: A hand with two chows in the same suit with numbers 1, 2, 3 and 7, 8, 9. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Pung of Terminals or Honors: A hand with a pung or kong of terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) or honor tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Melded Kong: A hand with one exposed kong.\\n        * One Voided Suit: A hand with exactly two of the three suits of tiles.\\n        * No Honors: A hand with no honor tiles.\\n        * Edge Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile of number 3 to complete a chow of 1, 2, 3, or number 7 to complete a chow of 7, 8, 9. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Closed Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile to complete a chow as the middle number. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Single Wait: The winning tile must be the tile to complete a pair. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Self Drawn: The winning tile must be drawn by the winner.\\n    Now is your turn, to your best ability, please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format, such as Play B5.\\n        Your response format should be exactly:\\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\\n    \", \"user_prompt\": \"The following is the state information: The Prevailing Wind is: North Wind.Your Seat Wind is: South Wind.\\nUnshown tiles are: [W1: 4; W2: 4; W3: 4; W4: 1; W5: 4; W6: 4; W7: 3; W8: 3; W9: 3; T1: 4; T2: 2; T3: 2; T4: 3; T5: 3; T6: 4; T7: 2; T8: 3; T9: 4; B1: 3; B2: 3; B3: 2; B4: 3; B5: 0; B6: 4; B7: 3; B8: 3; B9: 1; F1: 0; F2: 0; F3: 1; F4: 2; J1: 1; J2: 3; J3: 3].\\nYour hand tiles are: [W1: 1; W2: 1; W3: 1; W5: 1; W6: 1; W7: 1; T4: 2; T5: 1; B7: 2].\\nYour melds are: [[T2, T3, T4]].\\nThe melds of the next player: [[W4, W4, W4]].\\nThe melds of the player sitting across: [[J1, J1, J1]].\\nThe melds of the previous player: [[W7, W8, W9]].\\nYour game history is: Play F3, Play B9, Play B3, Play T7, Play B5, Play T3, Play T7, Play B1, Play B5, Chi T3.\\nThe game history of the next player is: Play F3, Play F1, Play F4, Play F2, Play J1, Play J2, Play J3, Peng W4, Play B4, Play B8, Play B7.\\nThe game history of the player sitting across is: Play T8, Play W7, Play B2, Play T5, Peng J1, Play T2, Play F2, Play W4, Play B9, Play F1, Play B5.\\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play F3, Play F1, Chi W8, Play F4, Play F2, Play F2, Play B3, Play B5, Play F1, Play B9, Play T3.\\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play W1; Play B7; Play W2; Play W6; Play T4; Play W7; Play W5; Play W3; Play T5]\\u3002\", \"reasoning\": \"\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: My current hand has good potential for a Mixed Straight (8 points) or All Types (6 points) if I can complete chows in different suits. I have several pairs and sequences in progress, but my meld is a Chow [T2, T3, T4], and other players' melds show many winds and dragons already used, making some high-scoring patterns less likely. Unshown tiles indicate low availability of B5 (0), F1 (0), F2 (0), and some terminals/honors are scarce. My hand is mostly simple tiles (W1-7, T4-5, B7) with a pair of B7 and T4, and potential sequences like W1-W2-W3, W5-W6-W7, etc. Discarding a tile that preserves flexibility and keeps potential for Mixed Straight or All Types is key. Options include discarding a single honor (none here), a terminal (W1, W7, B7), or a middle tile that doesn't break promising sequences.\\n\\nPossible discards:\\n- Play W1: W1 is terminal, but it's part of a potential sequence W1-W2-W3; discarding it loses that chance, but I only have one of each, so it's weak. It's also a terminal, which could help Outside Hand if kept, but my hand is leaning toward simples.\\n- Play W2: Similar to W1, part of sequence but only one copy; discarding might be okay if I focus on other suits.\\n- Play W3: Same issue.\\n- Play W5: Middle tile, could be useful for sequences but currently isolated.\\n- Play W6: Part of potential W5-W6-W7 sequence; keeping might be better.\\n- Play W7: Terminal, part of potential sequence with W6, but I have only one.\\n- Play T4: I have two T4s, making a pair, and T4 is middle tile useful for chows; discarding breaks the pair and reduces flexibility.\\n- Play T5: Only one T5, but it's adjacent to T4 for potential chows.\\n- Play B7: I have two B7s, a pair, and B7 is terminal; discarding loses a pair and a terminal, but terminals might not help much given other patterns.\\n\\nBest to discard a tile that doesn't break pairs or key sequences, and that has low value for future draws. Given unshown tiles, W5, W6, T4, T5 have moderate availability, but B7 has 3 left, so keeping the pair might be good. W1, W2, W3 are terminals with few sequences possible since I have only singles; discarding one of them could be safe. W1 is least connected (only sequence with W2-W3, but I have no W4 and W4 is mostly used in next player's meld), so it's a candidate.\\n\\nReason: I choose to discard W1 because it's a terminal with limited sequence potential given my hand and other players' melds (W4 is scarce and used), and keeping other tiles like pairs (T4, B7) and potential sequences (W5-W6-W7, T4-T5) offers more flexibility for completing a winning hand, possibly aiming for Mixed Straight or All Types. Discarding W1 also reduces terminal count, aligning with All Simples (2 points) if I avoid honors and terminals.\\n\\nAnswer: Play W1\"}"}
